<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1903</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hay-Bunau-Varilla Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty was signed on 18 November 1903 by US Secretary of State John Hay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-bunau-varilla">Philippe Bunau-Varilla</a>, acting for the newly independent Panama. It granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in perpetuity a zone ten miles wide across the isthmus to build and run a canal, in return for 10 million dollars and an annual payment, and guaranteed Panama's independence. Its terms became a long-standing grievance and were replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a> of 1977.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hay-bunau-varilla-treaty.jpg" alt="Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown artist for New York Times · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panama_canal_cartoon_1903.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
